.TH STURY 1 "2026-10-26" "stury 0.0.2" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
stury \- JSON and CSV codec with a jq 1.8.2\-compatible command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stury
[\-cCMnRrsSaejV] [\-\-tab|\-\-indent n] [\-\-arg name value] [\-\-argjson name value] [\-\-slurpfile name file] [\-\-rawfile name file] [\-f file] [\-L dir] [\-\-seq] [\-\-stream] [\-\-from format] [\-\-to format] [\-\-grow\-header] [\-\-max\-depth n] [stury options] <jq filter> [file...]
.br
.B stury
.B check
[\-\-from format|\-\-seq] [\-\-strict\-limits] [\-\-max\-depth n] [file...]
.br
.B stury
fmt
[\-\-from format] [\-\-to format] [\-\-in\-place|\-\-check] [\-\-indent n|\-\-tab] [\-\-max\-depth n] [file...]
.br
.B stury
\-h | \-V | \-\-build\-configuration
.SH DESCRIPTION
stury applies a jq 1.8 filter to JSON inputs and gives jq's results and exit codes. Its messages start with stury: and are worded its own way. It accepts all of jq 1.8's options except \-\-run\-tests, a test\-file runner it does not have. The filter language and those options are described in
.BR jq (1).
This page covers stury's own options and how its input handling differs from jq's.
.PP
.B stury check
(or
.BR validate )
validates its inputs, accepting any input that
.B stury .
reads without error, and prints nothing when they are valid. It requires at least one JSON text: an empty or whitespace-only input is "No JSON text" and exits 5. It is the subcommand when it is the first argument that is not an option. With no file on a terminal it prints the short usage and exits 2.
.PP
.B stury fmt
writes each input back in the printer's layout, or in another format: it runs no filter, reads each file as
\-\-from and writes it as \-\-to (the same format by default). JSON and JSONC are laid out as stury prints them, comments kept; ndjson is
one compact value per line. Between these formats only the layout changes: each number, string and key is written
as it was, and every member of an object is kept, a repeated key included. csv and tsv are a header and one record
per row, re-spelled by the cell writer, each file a table of its own. A leading byte order mark is not written, and
nesting past 1024 levels is refused with exit 5. The run is idempotent, and a file already in that layout comes
back byte for byte.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-from " format"
Read json (the default), jsonc, ndjson, csv or tsv. With check, judge the input as that format is read.
.TP
.BI \-\-to " format"
Write json (the default), jsonc, ndjson (as \-c), csv or tsv. csv and tsv write each output as one record. An object is a row under a header of the first object's keys (sorted under \-S). A missing key is an empty cell, and an extra key ends the run with exit 5. An array or a scalar is a row with no header, and the two kinds do not mix. A string is its text, a number is spelled as jq spells it, and null is an empty cell. csv writes "" as a quoted empty cell; tsv cannot tell the two apart. An array or object in a cell ends the run (tojson makes text of it). Not with \-r, \-j, \-a, \-C, \-c, \-\-indent, \-\-tab, \-\-seq or \-\-raw\-output0, and not with check.
.TP
.B \-\-grow\-header
With \-\-to csv or tsv, hold the output and write a header of every key in the order first seen (byte order under \-S). Memory holds all the output.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " ndjson"
Read one JSON text per line, and with \-\-to ndjson print compact, one value per line, so \-\-indent, \-\-tab and \-\-seq are refused with it.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " jsonc"
Read JSONC (comments and trailing commas), also for \-\-argjson, \-\-jsonargs, \-\-slurpfile (one document per file) and fromjson. Values print with their comments.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " csv"
Read CSV (RFC 4180). The first record of each file names the fields. Each later record is one input, an object of those names with each cell's text as a string, never a number. An empty cell with no quotes is null and a quoted "" is ""; every empty tsv cell is null. A repeated header name, a record of another width and a fault in the text end the file with NAME:LINE:COLUMN: FAULT and exit 5. A file up to 64 MiB is read whole. A larger file, a pipe and a file under \-n are read a window at a time, and a window holds the longest record. A filter that is only columns (.name, .a, .b, or [.a, .b] under \-c) is printed from the cells in pieces on every core. With \-s the records are one array. Not with \-R, \-\-seq or \-\-stream.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " tsv"
Read tab\-separated values as \-\-from csv does, with no quoting and the escapes \\t, \\n, \\r, \\0 and \\\\ that jq's @tsv writes.
.TP
.B \-\-in\-place
With \fBfmt\fR, rewrite each file through a temporary file in its directory and a rename. Mode (and owner and group where permitted) are kept; other hard links keep the old bytes. Only when \-\-to is \-\-from; refused with \-\-check, with standard input, and on a non\-regular file (exit 2).
.TP
.B \-\-check
With \fBfmt\fR, write nothing: exit 1 when any file's formatted bytes differ from its bytes, 0 when every file
is already formatted. A fault still exits 5.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-depth " n"
Refuse nesting past n levels (default 1024; jq's is 10000).
.TP
.B \-\-strict\-limits
With \fBcheck\fR, keep the library's limits: numbers of at most 256 digits and a scale of at most 4096.
.TP
.B \-V
Print stury\- and the version it was built with: a release version, or the short commit hash from a source build (dev outside a checkout).
.SH INPUT DIFFERENCES FROM JQ
stury reads strict RFC 8259 JSON. It rejects, with exit 5, these inputs that jq accepts: 01, 1., NaN, nan, Infinity, invalid UTF\-8 in a string, a lone low surrogate escape, a NUL after a value, and nesting past \-\-max\-depth. A parse error names the fault and the position of the byte where it was found. Each file ends its texts, except under \-\-seq. The full list is in docs/cli.md, "Differences from jq".
.SH EXIT STATUS
As jq: 1 under \-e when the last output is false or null, 2 for a usage or file error, 3 for a filter that does not compile, 4 under \-e when there is no output, 5 for invalid input or an error on the last input. \fBfmt \-\-check\fR exits 1 when a file would change.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR jq (1),
docs/cli.md in stury's source.
